y to benefit humanity\nL87: \nL88: cite26†About Google DeepMind L89: \nL90: Learn more\nL91: \nL92: cite29†Education cite30†Our National Partnerships for AI cite31†Accelerator programs cite32†The Podcast cite33†DeepMind Institute†institute.deepmind.com L93: \nL94: cite15†Responsibility Ensuring AI safety through proactive security, even against evolving threats cite27†News Discover our latest AI breakthroughs, projects, and updates cite28†Careers We’re looking for people who want to make a real, positive impact on the world L95: cite34†Build with Gemini†aistudio.google.com cite35†Try Gemini†gemini.google.com L96: \nL97: cite36†Google DeepMind L98: \nL99: cite37†Google AI Learn about all our AI†ai.google cite38†Google DeepMind Explore the frontier of AI cite39†Google Labs Try our AI experiments†labs.google cite40†Google Research Explore our research†research.google L100: \nL101: Products and apps\nL102: cite35†Gemini app Chat with Gemini†gemini.google.com cite34†Google AI Studio Build with our next-gen AI models†aistudio.google.com cite41†Google Antigravity Our agentic development platform†antigravity.google L103: \nL104: cite0†Models L105: \nL106: cite10†Research L107: \nL108: cite17†Science L109: \nL110: cite26†About L111: \nL112: cite34†Build with Gemini†aistudio.google.com cite35†Try Gemini†gemini.google.com L113: \nL114: September 25, 2025 Models\nL115: # Gemini Robotics 1.5 brings AI agents into the physical world\nL116: \nL117: Carolina Parada\nL118: \nL119: cite42†Image: Three vertical panels showing Gemini Robotics 1.5 performing physical tasks: a white robotic arm holding green tape, a mechanical claw placing red grapes into a green container, and Apollo, the white Apptronik humanoid robot.†lh3.googleusercontent.com L120: \nL121: We’re powering an era of physical agents — enabling robots to perceive, plan, think, use tools and act to better solve complex, multi-step tasks.\nL122: Earlier this year, we made incredible progress bringing cite1†Gemini 's multimodal understanding into the physical world, starting with the cite43†Gemini Robotics family of models.\nL123: \nL124: Today, we’re taking another step towards advancing intelligent, truly general-purpose robots. We're introducing two models that unlock agentic experiences with advanced thinking:\nL125:   * cite44†Gemini Robotics 1.5 – Our most capable vision-language-action (VLA) model turns visual information and instructions into motor commands for a robot to perform a task. This model thinks before taking action and shows its process, helping robots assess and complete complex tasks more transparently. It also learns across embodiments, accelerating skill learning.\nL126:   * cite45†Gemini Robotics-ER 1.5 – Our most capable vision-language model (VLM) reasons about the physical world, natively calls digital tools and creates detailed, multi-step plans to complete a mission. This model now achieves state-of-the-art performance across spatial understanding benchmarks.\nL127: These advances will help developers build more capable and versatile robots that can actively understand their environment to complete complex, multi-step tasks in a general way.\nL128: \nL129: Starting today, we’re making Gemini Robotics-ER 1.5 available to developers via the Gemini API in cite46†Google AI Studio†aistudio.google.com . Gemini Robotics 1.5 is currently available to select partners. Read more about building with the next generation of physical agents cite47†on the Developer blog†developers.googleblog.com .\nL130: ## Gemini Robotics 1.5: Unlocking agentic experiences for physical tasks\nL131: \nL132: Most daily tasks require contextual information and multiple steps to complete, making them notoriously challenging for robots today.\nL133: For example, if a robot was asked, “Based on my location, can you sort these objects into the correct compost, recycling and trash bins?\" it would need to search for relevant local recycling guidelines on the internet, look at the objects in front of it and figure out how to sort them based on those rules — and then do all the steps needed to completely put them away. So, to help robots complete these types of complex, multi-step tasks, we designed two models that work together in an agentic framework.\nL134: Our embodied reasoning model, Gemini Robotics-ER 1.5, orchestrates a robot’s activities, like a high-level brain. This model excels at planning and making logical decisions within physical environments. It has state-of-the-art spatial understanding, interacts in natural language, estimates its success and progress, and can natively call tools like cite48†Google Search†search.google to look for information or use any third-party user-defined functions.\nL135: Gemini Robotics-ER 1.5 then gives Gemini Robotics 1.5 natural language instructions for each step, which uses its vision and language understanding to directly perform the specific actions. Gemini Robotics 1.5 also helps the robot think about its actions to better solve semantically complex tasks, and can even explain its thinking processes in natural language — making its decisions more transparent.\n"
